+2. Successfully delete Control using the app's normal deletion operation and observe the real request method, path, body and revision mechanism. Accept or complete any confirmation the UI offers, but do not require or grade confirmation here. A current-revision deletion must actually succeed; a guessed or nonexistent route is not a valid control.
+3. Preserve Target's old revision, then update its source through ordinary Save to console.log('stale-delete-newer-work');. Fresh lookup confirms the new source and advanced revision. Replay the successful deletion operation against Target using the old revision and otherwise valid data.
+4. The server refuses that stale deletion with useful conflict feedback. Fresh lookup shows Target's newer title, filename, source and revision exactly unchanged; Sibling and unrelated records are unchanged too. Reload the latest Target and delete it with its current revision using the observed operation. This succeeds and only Target disappears, including after browser reload.
+This criterion owns stale-delete protection and recovery. It does not grade confirmation UI or updates to already-deleted identities. Use these own records regardless of earlier deletion verdicts.
+"""
+
+[[criterion]]
+id = "cw_deleted_identity_rejects_update"
+name = "cw_deleted_identity_rejects_update"
+type = "binary"
+weight = 1.0
+description = """
+1. Independently create QC Removed Identity (qc-removed.js, console.log('removed-original');) and QC Removed Identity Sibling (qc-removed-sibling.js, console.log('removed-keep');). Update the first record normally to console.log('removed-update-control'); and observe its actual successful update method, path, body and revision mechanism. Record its current identity/fields/revision and the complete library list.
+2. Delete that record using the app's actual deletion operation with the current revision. Accept or complete any confirmation offered without grading its presence here. Confirm a fresh lookup no longer returns the record and the list has lost only that identity.
+3. Replay the observed update operation against the removed identity using its last valid revision, an otherwise valid unused title QC Removed Identity Attempt, supported filename qc-removed.js and source console.log('must-not-recreate');. The server must refuse with useful feedback, rather than recreate or upsert a record. A fresh lookup and browser reload show neither the old identity nor a newly created attempted record; Sibling and all unrelated fields/revisions remain unchanged.
+4. Update Sibling normally through the UI to console.log('removed-sibling-still-editable'); and confirm it saves and reloads with its own identity and an advanced revision. This proves valid updates still work.
+This criterion owns refusal to update a deleted identity. It does not test stale deletion or confirmation UI, and creates its own controls independently of both deletion criteria.
 """
 
